apple's next official event is now  rumored to be coming on march 8th which  is less than a month away from today and  that is extremely exciting since we've  been basically going through a little  tech drought over the holidays and into  the new year and as far as what we can  expect to be revealed at the event next  month we're definitely going to be  getting a couple of very boring updates  to the ipad air and the iphone se but  other than that mark german did say that  apple plans to launch at least one new  mac this spring and if you're wondering  if this could be the new redesigned m2  macbook air that we've recently showed  off in these beautiful renders don't get  excited because mark said that those m2  max will likely launch later in the year  so with that said i actually believe  that we are now 100 guaranteed to get a  new redesigned mac mini at apple's event  next month and before i dig deep into  all the leaks and rumors and my thoughts  on the design and how it's all gonna  work let me first go through all of the  evidence that points to a launch next  month first of all within mark german's  report he said that an apple silicon  replacement for the 27 inch imac is not  expected to launch until as late as  august or september which is really  unfortunate and with the imac out of the  way the other option is potentially the  new mac pro which everyone is expecting  to get released later in the year like  dylan dkt who said he expects it to  launch in the fourth quarter which makes  perfect sense to me so that simply  leaves the high-end mac mini that's next  up for a redesign and update and we know  this for sure because it's one of the  only macs that is still relying on an  intel cpu so it 100 needs to get  replaced before apple's two-year  timeline for the apple silicon  transition is over and on top of that  apple has already released their m1 pro  and m1 max chips which are the ones that  are expected to go into this new mac  mini so there's no hold up on the  hardware side but going even deeper john  prosser from front page tech was the one  who first revealed the renders for the  mac mini last year and he said it could  come as early as the end of 2021 and  that's actually what a bunch of other  analysts believed like dylan dkt who was  pretty sure the mac mini would be  released alongside the new 14 and 16  inch macbook pros but unfortunately it  didn't happen because apple wanted the  absolute most attention on the macbooks  instead of sharing it with the mac mini  and if this is true then it's very  likely that the mac mini could have been  ready to launch late last year but it's  just been sitting there on a shelf  waiting for another apple event so that  they could launch it because whenever  apple gives a product a major redesign  they almost always choose to show it off  at an event to get the maximum amount of  press coverage and if any product  redesign deserves a slot in a dedicated  apple event it's hands down the mac mini  because believe it or not the general  shape and design has stayed almost  identical since 2010. yes that means it  kept the same chassis for almost 12 full  years which is mind-blowing and i don't  think this has ever happened to any  other apple product so it obviously  deserves to be shown off at an event and  i'm gonna go ahead and say it it's gonna  happen at next month's event a hundred  percent guaranteed no doubt about it and  the reason this makes sense is because  apple still has a lot of macs to release  this year and only a handful of  potential events to show off these new  designs which include a full redesign  for the high-end imac a new mac pro a  new redesigned macbook air and of course  the mac mini so without further ado  let's jump right into all the nitty  gritty details about the mac mini  redesign and what we should expect from  it john prosser's original renders  created by ian's elbow showed off a much  thinner mac mini with rounded off edges  at the top but the most curious part of  the design is that apple is choosing to  give it a plexiglas top now this detail  is important because having a plastic  top will instantly make it easier for  the bluetooth and wi-fi antennas to get  a stronger signal because if you didn't  already know people have been having  connectivity issues because of the full  metal redesign that we got many years  ago and if you think the whole plexiglas  thing is wild it's actually not because  that's exactly what the original mac  mini came with so it seems like apple is  bringing the old design back and just to  confirm this detail leaks apple pro on  twitter shared some leaked cad files  that showed off a metal chassis with a  completely separate top piece that  matches john's renders and this detail  is going to be very important to the  cooling aspect which i'll get back to in  just a minute now moving around to the  ports these seem to be directly from the  leaked cad files and it's definitely an  awesome and exciting sign because it  looks like we'll be getting four  thunderbolt 4 ports which is a very nice  addition on the mac mini however some of  this doesn't really make much sense like  how these ports are way too close to  each other compared to apple's usual  spacing on their recent 24 inch imac and  the weirdest thing of all is how this  render is missing the headphone jack  which is present on every single new mac  model out there and it's even more  important on the mac mini since it's a  desktop machine that you can hook up to  external speakers so apple insider  decided to make their own render of the  new redesigned mac mini by apple  tomorrow on twitter which i've gotta say  look really really good and clean and  they even made a new render that adds  that headphone jack back in to see what  it would look like and i think the  thunderbolt spacing makes much more  sense here now i do expect this model to  come with the option of a 10 gigabit  ethernet port just like the current m1  mac mini and it looks like it's getting  the new magnetic power connector which i  absolutely love on the new m1 imac and  this detail is extremely important  because when apple added that new  magnetic port to the 24 inch imac they  also removed the internal power supply  and gave it a bulky external power brick  and the point that i'm trying to make is  that if apple was willing to remove the  internal power supply on the much larger  imac then why wouldn't they do the same  thing with the mac mini since they  probably already produced a ton of those  external bricks and if they do end up  doing this it would save them a massive  amount of internal space on the inside  of the mac mini for example if you look  at a tear down of the current m1 mac  mini the actual logic board that houses  the m1 chip and the cooling system is  tiny compared to the size of the chassis  itself and a pretty big chunk of the  design is taken up by the power supply  alone so if the newly redesigned mac  mini ditches the internal power supply  then there is going gonna be a lot of  room on the inside for a bigger logic  board and more importantly a bigger  cooling system now moving over to the  bottom side john showed off a completely  flat bottom with these nice looking  rubber pads and i think this totally  makes sense but the interesting detail  is that the exhaust vent that's  currently on the back of the m1 mac mini  has now been moved over to the bottom  and this also makes sense because the  new mac mini is so much thinner than  before but the big question this leads  up to is how in the world is the cooling  system going to work because with the  previous design the mac mini sat on a  rubber circular pad and air would  literally flow in through the crack  around the edges of that pad which is  genius engineering but looking at the  new design renders the bottom looks like  it's sealed apart from the exhaust vent  and even if there was an intake vent it  would be a bad idea for it to be on the  bottom because it could end up pulling  hot air from that bottom facing exhaust  vent but if you really put some thought  into it and take a close look at the  renders you'll see that there is a  perfect opportunity for apple to add a  thin crack all around the edge of the  plexiglass top and that can act as the  intake similar to how the current intake  works but on the top and located towards  the front and this would actually work  incredibly well because the air would  flow through the entire chassis from the  front to the back and through the  exhaust vents for optimal cooling of the  chips and speaking of the chips i don't  expect it to get anything more than the  current m1 pro and m1 max chips that we  already have on the macbook pros so the  performance should be identical to those  laptops and in terms of the pricing i  personally expected to come in at 1299  for the base model with the m1 pro chip  which will likely be binned just like  the base 14 inch macbook pro and to be  honest that's still a very good deal  considering the amount of power and  optimization that you're getting now if  you disagree with my idea of the  redesigned mac mini getting revealed at  next month's apple event then let me  know your thoughts down in the comment  section below but if you enjoyed this  video go ahead and click the circle  above to subscribe for more videos like  this one thanks for watching and we'll  see you in the next video  [Music]

